// TEMPLATE — copy this file to `Whoopsy.local.xcconfig` in this same directory and fill in the
// values you have. The copy is gitignored; this template is committed.
//
//     cp ios/App/Config/Whoopsy.local.xcconfig.example ios/App/Config/Whoopsy.local.xcconfig
//
// Then edit `ios/App/Config/Whoopsy.local.xcconfig` and put each value after its `=`. Nothing else
// needs to change: `ios/App/Config/Whoopsy.xcconfig` already ends with
//
//     #include? "Whoopsy.local.xcconfig"
//
// which is the optional include, so the file is picked up as soon as it exists and its absence is
// not a build error. **Leave a line empty and that feature stays off** — an empty `MBX_ACCESS_TOKEN`
// draws `MapKit` instead of the offline map, and an empty pair of sync settings means the app never
// opens a socket.
//
// ## `MBX_ACCESS_TOKEN` — the public Mapbox token
//
// The PUBLIC (`pk.…`) token — the one Mapbox shows on your account's Tokens page as the default
// public token. It ships inside every built app and is designed to be embedded; it is untracked here
// at the owner's request, not because it is dangerous.
//
// Do NOT put the SECRET (`sk.…`) token here. That one is scoped `Downloads:Read` and is a build-time
// credential for SwiftPM's package resolution — it belongs in `~/.netrc` only, with `chmod 0600`,
// and never in this repository, in `Info.plist`, or in the app. `MapboxSetup.configure()` refuses
// anything that does not begin `pk.`, so pasting it here fails loudly rather than shipping it.
//
// ## `WHOOPSY_API_BASE_URL` and `WHOOPSY_API_TOKEN` — the cloud sync, or neither
//
// Both, or neither. The Worker refuses every `/v1` request without a credential, so a base URL on
// its own is an install that opens a socket to collect a `401`; `DIContainer` treats that pair as
// unconfigured and installs `UnconfiguredCloudSync` instead.
//
// `WHOOPSY_API_BASE_URL` is the deployed Worker's origin, with no trailing slash and no path —
//
//     https://whoopsy-sync.<your-account-subdomain>.workers.dev
//
// **Write it with `$()` standing in for the second slash**, exactly as the line at the foot of this
// file does:
//
//     https:/$()/whoopsy-sync.<your-account-subdomain>.workers.dev
//
// That is not superstition. `//` opens a comment in an xcconfig value, so the plain spelling is read
// as `https:` and the build succeeds with a base URL that cannot make a request — the failure is a
// socket error at runtime and nothing at build time says so. An empty `$()` expands to nothing, so the
// two slashes never sit next to each other in the file and the expansion puts them back.
//
// `WHOOPSY_API_TOKEN` is **the deployment's shared secret**, the same string in three homes:
//
//     backend/.dev.vars                      `SYNC_API_TOKEN=`      — for `npm run dev`
//     ios/App/Config/Whoopsy.local.xcconfig  `WHOOPSY_API_TOKEN=`   — for the app (this file)
//     `npx wrangler secret put SYNC_API_TOKEN`                      — for the deployment
//
// Generate it once with `openssl rand -base64 32` and use that one value everywhere. **Set the
// deployment's copy before you run `wrangler deploy`** — a deploy does not fail on a missing
// secret, it ships a Worker that refuses everything.
//
// **This token is not a secret in the strong sense, and the app is where that shows.** It is
// compiled into the binary and can be extracted from it, so it stops scanners and drive-by requests
// against a discoverable hostname and does not authenticate a person. It is still worth keeping out
// of the repository: the same string is the deployment's credential, and a leaked one is a
// deployment anyone can read.
//
// ## The lines to edit
//
// Uncomment and fill in — an `=` with nothing after it is what the committed base file already
// sets, so leaving this file empty is the same as not having it at all:

# MBX_ACCESS_TOKEN = pk.your_public_token_here

# WHOOPSY_API_BASE_URL = https:/$()/whoopsy-sync.your-account-subdomain.workers.dev
# WHOOPSY_API_TOKEN = paste_the_output_of_openssl_rand_base64_32_here
